{"causes":["Emergence of classical Indian culture in ancient India (400 BCE - 500 CE)","Influence of Hinduism on Indian performing arts (3000 BCE - 1500 CE)","Development of traditional Indian dance forms (1000 BCE - present)"],"effects":["Influence on Indian classical music and dance (500 CE - present)","Shaping of traditional Indian theater and performance arts (400 BCE - present)","Inspiration for contemporary artists and scholars (1960s - present)"],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Bharata Muni","sourceName":"Bharata Muni","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"Natya Shastra","targetName":"Natya Shastra","context":"Founded the Natya Shastra, a comprehensive guide to performing arts."},{"sourceSlug":"Hinduism","sourceName":"Hinduism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Natya Shastra","targetName":"Natya Shastra","context":"Influenced the development of Indian performing arts through its philosophical and cultural traditions."},{"sourceSlug":"India","sourceName":"India","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Natya Shastra","targetName":"Natya Shastra","context":"Native to ancient India, where it was first composed by Bharata Muni."},{"sourceSlug":"Classical Indian Culture","sourceName":"Classical Indian Culture","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Natya Shastra","targetName":"Natya Shastra","context":"Played a significant role in shaping the cultural landscape of ancient India."},{"sourceSlug":"Indian Classical Music","sourceName":"Indian Classical Music","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Natya Shastra","targetName":"Natya Shastra","context":"Evolved from the principles outlined in Natya Shastra."},{"sourceSlug":"Bharatanatyam","sourceName":"Bharatanatyam","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Natya Shastra","targetName":"Natya Shastra","context":"A traditional Indian dance form that was influenced by Natya Shastra."},{"sourceSlug":"Kathakali","sourceName":"Kathakali","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Natya Shastra","targetName":"Natya Shastra","context":"Another traditional Indian dance form that was shaped by Natya Shastra."},{"sourceSlug":"Contemporary Artists","sourceName":"Contemporary Artists","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Natya Shastra","targetName":"Natya Shastra","context":"Find inspiration in the principles and concepts outlined in Natya Shastra."}],"places":[{"name":"Ancient India","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"India","role":"Major location"},{"name":"Kerala","role":"Regional significance"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Natya Shastra","type":"Reference article","slug":"natya-shastra"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q550599","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natya_Shastra"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Natya Shastra contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Nāṭyaśāstra (Sanskrit: नाट्यशास्त्र, 'Treatise on Theatre/Drama') is an ancient Indian Sanskrit treatise on the performing arts — drama, dance, and music — attributed to the sage Bharata Muni and composed c. 200 BCE – 200 CE (dates are debated, with scholars placing the core text anywhere from the 2nd century BCE to the 4th century CE). It is the most comprehensive and authoritative ancient Indian treatise on the performing arts, covering the entire range of theatrical performance: the origin of drama (the 'fifth Veda', said to have been created by Brahma), the construction of the theatre (ranga), stagecraft (bhūmikā), acting technique (abhinaya), the theory of rasa (aesthetic emotion), music (svara), dance (nṛtta), costume, makeup, and the 36 subsidiary elements of theatrical production. The Nāṭyaśāstra contains approximately 6,000 verses across 36 chapters.

The Nāṭyaśāstra's most influential theoretical contribution is the Rasa theory — the analysis of the aesthetic experience of drama (and by extension all art) in terms of eight (later nine) rasas ('flavours', 'essences', 'aesthetic emotions'): śṛṅgāra (love, romance), hāsya (humour, comedy), karuṇa (pathos, sorrow), raudra (fury, wrath), vīra (heroism, courage), bhayānaka (terror, fear), bībhatsa (disgust, repugnance), adbhuta (wonder, amazement), and the later addition of śānta (peace, serenity). Each rasa is produced by specific determinants (vibhāva), consequents (anubhāva), and transitory emotions (vyabhicāribhāva) — a systematic analysis of how theatrical performance produces specific aesthetic responses in the spectator.

The Rasa theory became the foundational framework of Indian aesthetics — applied not only to drama and dance but to poetry (kāvya), music (sangīta), and the visual arts — and continues to be the primary theoretical framework for classical Indian performing arts, influencing the major systems of classical Indian dance (Bharatanatyam, Kathak, Odissi, Manipuri, Kuchipudi, Mohiniyattam, Kathakali, Sattriya) and the traditions of Indian classical theatre and music.","subjects":["Sanskrit Literature","Ancient Era","Indian Performing Arts","Aesthetics","Drama Theory","Indian Philosophy","Dance Theory","Classical India"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Natya Shastra had a lasting impact on traditional Indian dance and theater, shaping the development of classical Indian music and influencing contemporary artists.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
